# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings, on every checkout, on every platform.
#
# This is not tidiness. The completion feature shipped a Windows-shaped defect of
# exactly this kind — a CRLF-terminated activation line written into a profile,
# which bash reads as part of the command and chokes on (`install.rs`, "A
# CRLF-terminated activation line does not merely look untidy there"). A `.sh`
# file that reaches a Unix box with CRLF fails the same way and worse: the
# shebang line becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the error is
# `bash\r: command not found`, which names neither the file nor the cause.
#
# Developers on this repo work on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without
# this line the working copy drifts to CRLF and only the checkout settings of
# whoever commits next decide what lands in the blob. `eol=lf` takes that
# decision away from the local config.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts get LF too, and for the opposite reason: nothing breaks.
#
# PowerShell reads CRLF and LF alike, so `install.ps1` has no failure to point
# at — which is exactly why it needs a rule. Without one, the same
# `core.autocrlf=true` drift applies, and the bytes raw.githubusercontent serves
# to every Windows user running the documented one-liner become a function of
# one developer's local git config. `eol=lf` makes the blob, the working copy
# and what the CDN hands out the same file.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
